+# Why every field is a pill — and the real fix
+
+## The mechanism: who consumes `--radius-field`
+
+Scaffold-ETH 2 styles its UI with **DaisyUI 5**, running on **Tailwind CSS v4**.
+DaisyUI themes are declared with `@plugin "daisyui/theme" { ... }`, and each theme
+exposes three radius **design tokens**:
+
+- `--radius-selector` — small controls: checkbox, radio, toggle, badge
+- `--radius-field` — **field-family controls: `input`, `select`, `textarea`, `file-input`, `btn`, `tab`, range thumb**
+- `--radius-box` — containers: card, modal, alert
+
+These are not decorative. DaisyUI's component CSS hard-references them, e.g.:
+
+```css
+.input, .select, .textarea, .btn, .tab {
+  border-radius: var(--radius-field);
+}
+```
+
+So `--radius-field` is the **single source of truth** for the corner radius of
+every text field, select, and textarea in the app. Setting it to `9999rem` means
+every one of those components gets `border-radius: 9999rem`. When the radius
+exceeds half the element's height, the corners merge into a full semicircle →
+**pill shape**.
+
+**Why textareas clip their text:** a textarea is tall and multi-line, but the
+`9999rem` radius still applies to all four corners. Each corner becomes a huge
+arc that bites deep into the box; combined with the element's clipping, text (and
+the scrollbar/placeholder) near the corners is cut off. It's the same radius
+token doing damage on a tall box instead of a short one.
+
+## Why the per-component `rounded-*` patching keeps losing
+
+The teammate's `rounded-lg` patches aren't fighting a specificity bug — they
+"work" on the two inputs they were added to. They lose because they treat the
+**symptom on individual elements while the cause — the token — is unchanged**:
+
+1. **The default is still wrong.** `--radius-field: 9999rem` is the app-wide
+   default for the entire field family. Every *new* `input`/`select`/`textarea`
+   inherits it and comes out pill-shaped unless someone remembers to hand-annotate
+   it. It's whack-a-mole: the baseline regenerates the bug on every new field.
+
+2. **Patched fields pin a hardcoded value and drift.** `rounded-lg` bakes in
+   `0.5rem` at the call site, decoupled from the theme token. It no longer tracks
+   the design system, so as the rest of the app's intended radius is set
+   elsewhere, the manually-pinned fields drift out of sync ("out of style with
+   the rest"). You've replaced one source of truth with N scattered literals.
+
+The correct layer to fix this is the **token**, not the consumers.
+
+## The fix
+
+**Set `--radius-field` to a sane, finite radius** — the value the teammate was
+already reaching for with `rounded-lg`, so patched and unpatched fields agree:
+
+```css
+--radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* == Tailwind rounded-lg */
+```
+
+(Use `0.25rem`/`0.375rem` if you want subtler corners — it's a design choice.
+The point is a small finite value, not `9999rem`.)
+
+**Every place in `globals.css` it must be applied:** DaisyUI declares one theme
+per `@plugin "daisyui/theme"` block, and Scaffold-ETH 2 ships **two** — `light`
+and `dark`. The `9999rem` value lives in **both** blocks. Fixing only `light`
+leaves dark mode pill-shaped. So set `--radius-field: 0.5rem` in **the `light`
+theme block and the `dark` theme block** (i.e. every `@plugin "daisyui/theme"`
+block in the file).
+
+```css
+@plugin "daisyui/theme" {
+  name: "light";
+  ...
+  --radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* was 9999rem */
+  ...
+}
+
+@plugin "daisyui/theme" {
+  name: "dark";
+  ...
+  --radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* was 9999rem */
+  ...
+}
+```
+
+**Then remove the `rounded-lg` overrides** from the two patched inputs. With the
+token corrected, those fields inherit the right radius automatically and stay in
+sync with every future field — no per-component annotation needed.
+
+### Notes
+- Leave `--radius-selector` and `--radius-box` alone unless they're also
+  `9999rem` — they control different component families and aren't the reported
+  problem.
+- Verify both theme blocks in the real file; if the project defines more than two
+  themes, the same one-line change goes in each.

